            <ArticleTitle>One Ring to Fight Them All: The Sulfazecin Story.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2451-9456(17)30001-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.chembiol.2017.01.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In this issue of Cell Chemical Biology, Li et al. (2017) report on the biosynthesis of the monobactam sulfazecin by Pseudomonas acidophila and hypothesize a novel mechanism of β-lactam ring formation. As monobactam antibiotics are unaffected by some emerging resistance mechanisms (particularly metallo-β-lactamases), this discovery opens prospects to engineer β-lactam antibiotics against multi-drug resistant pathogens.</AbstractText>
